# Caddyfile — Sprout Automator reverse proxy (Caddy v2, mounted into the
# `caddy` service of docker-compose.prod.yml).
#
# Two modes, one file. Exactly one site block is active; comment the other out.
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MODE 1 — PUBLIC DOMAIN (the real deploy, phase-5 §5.4)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Point a DNS A record at the VPS, then activate this block (uncomment it and
# comment out the `localhost` block below). Caddy obtains and renews a real
# Let's Encrypt certificate automatically — no `tls` directive needed:
#
#   sprout.yourdomain.com {
#       encode gzip
#       reverse_proxy backend:3000
#   }
#
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# MODE 2 — IP / LOCAL (no public domain, or pre-VPS testing)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Active by default. The site address is a HOSTNAME (not a bare `:443`):
# Caddy's certificate automation only issues for named hostnames, so a bare
# `:443 { tls internal }` block serves nothing (handshakes fail with an
# "internal error" alert — there is no cert). `tls internal` makes Caddy issue
# a self-signed cert from its internal CA, so the full TLS path works with no
# DNS name. Reach the app at https://localhost/ and accept the self-signed
# cert (curl -k, or the browser's "not secure" bypass). On a LAN, replace
# `localhost` with the host's IP or LAN name (Caddy's internal CA issues for
# those too). This is what the phase-5 gate proves locally.

localhost {
	tls internal
	encode gzip
	reverse_proxy backend:3000
}
